{"about":"be248e4805a64ff3ba6394f7d33da88ef38fcbfe47a3829fdf587463136ac1c3","body":"The conjecture attempts to ground the preference for corroborated theories in verisimilitude (truth-likeness), yet Popper's own formal definition of verisimilitude was proven logically untenable by Tichý and Miller in 1974. They demonstrated that within Popper's framework, no false theory can be said to be closer to the truth than another false theory based solely on logical content and falsity content. By invoking verisimilitude as the justificatory mechanism, the conjecture leans on a concept that Popper himself admitted failed to provide the rigorous logical foundation required, thereby rendering the defense circular or dependent on an intuitive notion Popper explicitly tried to formalize and failed.","kind":"mini.criticism.v1","named":"The reliance on verisimilitude"}